  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "The first stable release, and the first to reach nuget.org: every version before\nit was a prerelease on GitHub Packages, a feed a consumer has to add and hold a\ntoken for. Nothing has been added to the scope 1.0.0-dev.3 reached - the same\nsix packages, the same public surface, member for member - so what 1.0.0 adds is\na commitment to that surface rather than anything new in it.\n\nOne tag versions every package in the repository, so all six are published\ntogether. The core carries a correctness fix to the conversion into\nSystem.Decimal and names the destination when a conversion out of the type\noverflows. One packaging change reaches all six: requireLicenseAcceptance is\ngone from every nuspec, so Visual Studio no longer asks a consumer to accept an\nApache-2.0 licence before installing. The other five packages carry no code\nchanges.\n\nPetToys.BigDecimal.Core\n\nFixed:\n- Converting to System.Decimal rounds once. The conversion used to round to\n  scale 28 and then give up the remaining digits one at a time, and each of\n  those steps decided its own tie from a remainder that no longer carried the\n  digits already dropped, so a result could come back a unit in the last place\n  away from the nearest decimal. It now computes the digits to give up and\n  rounds them in a single step, half to even, at the widest scale the value\n  fits. Measured against decimal itself over 200000 random values of 29 to 40\n  significant digits: of the 171976 a narrowing can represent, one in\n  thirty-three came back different before this change and none does now. Which\n  values overflow has not changed.\n\n  This reaches every route out of the type into decimal: the cast,\n  decimal.CreateChecked, CreateSaturating and CreateTruncating, and any database\n  column read into a decimal through one of the integration packages.\n\nChanged:\n- A checked conversion out of BigDecimal names the destination. Every one of\n  them used to report \"Value was either too large or too small for a\n  BigDecimal.\", which names the type the value came from rather than the type\n  that could not hold it, and that message is all the exception carries. A cast\n  or CreateChecked to decimal now reports \"... for a Decimal.\", to int \"... for\n  an Int32.\", to byte \"... for an unsigned byte.\", and so on for every\n  destination, in the wording the base class library uses for that type. The\n  refusal of NaN and the infinities names the destination the same way, in place\n  of the \"integer\" that named none of them. The exception type, the set of\n  values that throw, and every returned value are unchanged.",
